=== SURVEY ===

Pre-edit reading and grep'ing established:

1. The migration ladder. Latest existing migrations (in `migrations/`):
       000198_status_incidents.up.sql
       000199_si_aware_convert_functions.up.sql
       000200_alert_rule_message_template.up.sql
   The prompt's spec text says `000196_ai_settings.up.sql`, but 196 is taken
   (alert_rules_escalation). The next free number is 000201, used here.

2. The settings table is a typed key/value store, not the wide-column shape
   the prompt's `ALTER TABLE settings ADD COLUMN ai_mode TEXT…` snippet
   assumes. It has columns:
       key, value_text, value_num, value_bool, data_kind, updated_at
   per ADR-011. The wide-column ALTER would have created a parallel
   schema in conflict with the typed-struct facade (`SettingsRepo.Get`,
   `Upsert`, `applySettingsRow`). Adapted the design: add `value_jsonb`
   column, extend `data_kind` CHECK to allow 'jsonb', INSERT four rows
   (`ai_mode`, `ai_features`, `ai_provider_config`, `ai_cost_cap_cents`)
   with their default values. The Go struct and DTO surface remain
   identical to what the prompt described — only the storage shape is
   adapted to honour ADR-011.

3. TeslaSync is single-tenant per docs/INDEX.md and the existing
   `SettingsRepo.Get(ctx)` signature (no userID argument). Adjusted the
   `guard.Settings` interface to `AIMode(ctx)` / `AIFeatureEnabled(ctx, id)`
   without a userID parameter. The semantics are unchanged: both calls
   are read-only against the same key/value rows.

4. Existing chi router structure (internal/api/router.go) uses
   `r.Route("/api/v1", func(r chi.Router) { … })`; the new
   `mountAIRoutes(r, aiGuard)` plugs into that block alongside the
   `/watch` sub-router.

5. ESLint flat-config (`web/eslint.config.js`) imports the legacy
   `.eslintrc.cjs` via `@eslint/eslintrc`'s `FlatCompat` shim, plus a
   `jsx-a11y` block. The new `teslasync/ai-component-must-be-wrapped`
   rule is registered as a third top-level block scoped to
   `src/**/*.tsx`. The custom rule file uses ESM (`export default rule`)
   because `web/package.json` declares `"type": "module"`.

6. Vitest discovery: `vite.config.ts` includes `src/**/*.test.{ts,tsx}`.
   Hook tests in this repo live sibling-of-source (e.g.
   `useAuthMode.test.tsx` next to `useAuthMode.ts`) AND under
   `__tests__/` subdirs, both supported. F0 places `useAiEnabled.test.tsx`
   sibling to `useAiEnabled.ts`, and the broader off-mode invariant
   suite at `web/src/ai/__tests__/offMode.invariant.test.tsx`.

=== REASONING ===

This slice is the BLOCKING foundation for every later F/U/N/D/C/T/A/G/X/S/M/P/V/PU/GEN/ML feature in Phase-50. ADR-015 puts four invariants on the table that MUST hold before any AI work merges:

  I1 default-off:     fresh databases produce ai_mode='off'.
  I5 hidden UI:       no AI surface renders into the DOM in off mode.
  I6 404 routes:      every /api/v1/ai/* handler returns 404 in off mode.
  I7 type-system:     a slice that forgets to wrap its handler / component
                      cannot pass CI.

The design enforces each invariant in a *machine-checkable* way so future
slices cannot regress them by code-review oversight:

  - I1 — migration `000201_ai_settings` seeds `ai_mode='off'` (DEFAULT
    and INSERT both set it). `applySettingsRow` already has tested
    fall-through behaviour for missing rows (settings is a typed K/V
    store with per-key defaults). The Go `defaultSettings()` constant
    in `settings_repo.go` declares `AIMode: "off"`.

  - I5 — the SPA hook `useAiEnabled` returns `false` for every
    fail-closed condition (settings undefined, mode='off', flag missing,
    flag false). The HOC `withAiFeature` returns `null` when the hook
    returns false. The off-mode invariant Vitest suite walks every
    feature in `AI_FEATURE_IDS` and asserts no `[data-ai-feature]`
    attribute appears in the DOM.

  - I6 — the chi handler is wrapped by `guard.Wrap("chatbot-llm", h)`,
    which short-circuits with `http.NotFound` whenever
    `Settings.AIMode(ctx) == "off"`. The Go test
    `TestGuard_OffModeReturns404` exercises this directly against
    `httptest`. A second layer (`tools/aivet`) statically proves the
    wrap is in place — a bare `r.Post("/api/v1/ai/foo", h)` would fail
    CI before merge.

  - I7 — three independent guard rails:
      (a) tools/aivet — Go AST parse of `internal/api/*.go`, asserts
          every `/api/v1/ai/*` route is a Wrap call AND every
          `features.Registry[id].Routes.Backend` pattern is present in
          the router, AND `features.CoverageOK()` returns nil.
      (b) ESLint rule `teslasync/ai-component-must-be-wrapped` — file-
          path + name heuristic catches AI components whose default
          export is not the return value of `withAiFeature(...)`.
      (c) `tools/aigen --check` — fails CI if the TS mirror of the Go
          registry is out of sync, so a slice that adds a Go entry
          without regenerating the TS file cannot land.

The 404 (rather than 403/503) response code is intentional per
ADR-015 §I6: from the user's perspective the route does not exist. A
403 would leak the existence of an AI surface; a 503 would suggest a
transient outage and trigger client retries. Only an HTTP-level "this
URL is not part of this server" status preserves the invariant.

The single backend route mounted in F0 (`POST /api/v1/ai/chatbot`)
returns 501 Not Implemented when reached — *only after* a user has
opted in. This stub exists so the F0 verification can prove the
GUARD blocked the request rather than chi's default not-found
behaviour. Slice U1 (0011) replaces it with the real LLM-backed
handler.

